course: physics
lesson: 14
title: The air presses on everything
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: We live at the bottom of a deep ocean of air, and all that air above us has weight, so it presses on everything it touches.
explain: It presses from every side at once, up, down and sideways, on you, on the table and on the top of your juice.
explain: When you drink through a straw you do not really pull the juice up; you take some air out of the straw, and the air pressing on the juice in the cup pushes it up into your mouth.
explain: You never feel squashed, because the air and liquids inside your body push back just as hard, so the pushes balance.
explain: Many tricks, from a sucker on the tiles to a card holding water in an upside-down glass, are the outside air winning a pushing match.
fact: Air has weight; the air in an ordinary classroom weighs about as much as several children together.
fact: The air presses on every surface from all sides, and we do not feel it because our body pushes back just as hard.
fact: When you drink through a straw, you lower the air pressure inside it, and the air pressing on the drink in the cup pushes the drink up.
fact: A straw with a hole in its side stops working, because air leaks in and the pressure inside the straw cannot drop; and even a perfect pump cannot lift water more than about 10 metres up a pipe, because that is as far as the air can push it.
fact: On the Moon, where there is no air, drinking through a straw would not work at all.
fact: With a finger over the top of a straw, the drink stays in it, because the air below pushes up and no air can push down from above.
fact: A card can hold the water in an upside-down full glass, because the air pushes up on the card harder than the water pushes down.
fact: A sucker sticks because pressing it squeezes the air out from behind it, and the outside air then holds it on.
fact: Blowing more air into a balloon packs more air inside, which pushes harder on the rubber, so it grows bigger and tighter.
fact: High above the ground the air is thin and presses less; down low it presses harder, so a sealed crisp packet carried up a high mountain puffs up like a pillow.
fact: An empty bottle closed during a flight is squashed when the plane lands, because the thin air inside cannot push back against the stronger air at the ground.
fact: Your ears pop when the air pressure changes on a plane or in a fast lift, as a little tube lets air through to even out the push on both sides of the eardrum; swallowing, yawning or chewing helps it pop.
fact: Astronauts need pressurised suits (suits with air pressed in), because in space there is no air pressing on the outside of their bodies.
exercise: What really pushes the juice up a straw when you drink? | The air pressing on the juice in the cup | Your mouth pulling the juice up all by itself | The straw squeezing the juice upwards | The juice being lighter than air
exercise: Why does a straw with a hole in its side stop working? | Air leaks in, so the pressure inside cannot drop | The juice drains out through the hole | The hole makes the straw far too weak and floppy to hold juice | The hole lets the juice turn into bubbles
exercise: Would a straw work on the Moon, with no air at all? | No, there is no air to push the drink up | Yes, it would work even better there with no air | Yes, but only if you used a very short straw | Only if the drink were very cold and thick
exercise: You hold a finger over the top of a straw and lift it out. Why does the drink stay in? | The air pushes up from below and none pushes down from above | Your finger is sucking the drink up | The drink sticks to the inside of the straw | The straw is too narrow for any drink to fall
exercise: What happens when you then take your finger off? | Air rushes in from above and the drink falls out | The drink stays in there for ever, even with no finger on top | The drink shoots up out of the top | The straw fills with even more drink
exercise: A full glass with a card on top is turned upside down. Why does the water stay in? | The air pushes up on the card harder than the water pushes down | The water glues the card on with its wetness | The water becomes lighter when it is upside down | The card is magnetic and sticks to the glass
exercise: Why does a sucker stick to bathroom tiles? | The outside air holds it on after the air behind it is pushed out | The rubber is sticky like glue | The tiles pull on the rubber like a magnet | The water on the tiles freezes it in place
exercise: How do you get a sucker off the tiles easily? | Lift its edge so air rushes in behind it | Push it even harder against the tiles to loosen it | Pour cold water over the middle of it to shrink it | Pull it straight out from the middle with all your might
exercise: Why does a balloon get bigger and tighter as you blow? | More air packed inside pushes harder on the rubber | The rubber warms up from your breath and grows by itself | Your breath is lighter than the air outside | The air inside is pulling the rubber outwards
exercise: An empty bottle closed during a flight looks squashed after landing. Why? | The stronger air at the ground presses on the thin air inside | The bottle shrank in the cold air at the very top of the sky on the way | Someone sat on it in the luggage | The plastic melted in the warm airport
exercise: Why do your ears pop on a plane? | The air pressure changes and air evens out on both sides of the eardrum | The engine noise is too loud for your ears | Your ears freeze high up in the sky | The seat belt squeezes your head
exercise: Which of these helps your ears pop when landing? | Swallowing, yawning or chewing | Holding your breath and keeping still | Putting cotton wool deep inside your ears | Pressing hard on your ears with your fingers
exercise: About how much does the air in a classroom weigh? | About as much as several children | Nothing at all | About as much as a feather | About as much as a lorry full of bricks
exercise: Why are you not crushed by the push of all the air above you? | The air and liquids inside you push back just as hard | The air above you weighs nothing at all, so it cannot press | Your skin is too strong for air to press | The air only presses on the ground, not on people
exercise: Why do astronauts need pressurised suits in space? | There is no air outside to press on their bodies | Space air is far too heavy for people to breathe in | The suits stop them floating away | The suits make them look bigger on camera
exercise: Where does the air press harder, high on a mountain or down at the beach? | Down at the beach | High on the mountain | Both the same everywhere | It never presses anywhere
test: mate says air don't weigh nothing, it's just air. right? | No, air has weight, a classroom's air weighs as much as several kids | Yes, air is weightless | Yes, only wet air has weight | No, but only air inside a balloon weighs anything
test: A sealed crisp packet goes up a high mountain in your bag. What happens to it? | It puffs up like a pillow | It gets squashed flat | It stays exactly the same | It shrinks to half its size
test: In a cartoon a straw sucks up a whole pond from a long way off. What goes wrong in real life? | Even a perfect pump cannot lift water more than about 10 metres up a straw | Straws can lift water as high as you like | Real straws only work with cold water | Real straws work better the longer they are
test: Ugh my juice box straw keeps just gurgling, nothing comes up. What's the likely problem? | There's a hole or split letting air into the straw | The juice is too heavy to be pushed up such a thin straw | You need to blow hard into it first to get it going | Straws only work if you suck with your teeth closed tight
test: What would happen if you tried the upside-down card trick with a bent, floppy card and a wobble? | Air gets in and the water pours out | The water would still stay in for ever | The card would stick even harder | The water would float up out of the glass
test: How does a dropper or pipette keep liquid inside until you squeeze? | The same way a finger over a straw does: air pushes up from below | It has a tiny glue inside the tip | The liquid freezes inside the glass tube | Magnets in the bulb hold the liquid up
test: Your brother blows a balloon until it bangs. Why did it burst? | The rubber could not stretch any more against the air pushing out | The air inside suddenly turned heavy | The balloon got too cold to hold the air | Balloons always burst after a set number of breaths, however big they are
test: Which is true about the push of the air? | It presses on every side, up, down and sideways | It only presses downwards, from the sky above us onto the ground | It only presses on things that are outdoors, never indoors | It only presses on things when the wind is blowing hard
test: shut up about the sucker, it just sticks cos it's rubber innit | No, the outside air pushes it on once the air behind is squeezed out | Yes, rubber is naturally sticky on shiny tiles, just like a strip of tape | Yes, rubber and tiles are magnetic | No, it sticks because the tiles are cold
test: A football that is pumped up hard is weighed, then the air is let out. What changes? | It weighs a little less without the air | It weighs much more without the air | Its weight stays exactly the same | It weighs nothing at all with the air in
test: Why do your ears feel blocked and then pop in a lift going fast down a tall tower? | The air pressure grows quickly and the eardrum is pushed until air evens out | The lift is too noisy for your ears | The lift shakes the water in your ears | The lift's lights hurt your ears
exam: Identify the principal agency whereby a beverage is caused to ascend a drinking straw. | The pressure exerted by the surrounding atmosphere upon the beverage | An attractive force exerted by the mouth directly upon the liquid in the cup | The intrinsic buoyancy of the beverage in air | The elasticity of the material of the straw
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the atmosphere? | It possesses weight and exerts pressure upon all surfaces | It is entirely devoid of weight | It exerts pressure solely in a downward direction, from the sky towards the ground | It exerts pressure solely upon liquids
exam: It is given that a closed vessel is sealed at high altitude and subsequently conveyed to ground level. State the anticipated consequence. | The vessel is compressed by the greater external pressure | The vessel expands owing to the reduced external pressure at ground level | The vessel remains wholly unaltered | The vessel rises spontaneously
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the human body is not crushed by atmospheric pressure. | Internal pressures counterbalance the external pressure | The atmosphere exerts no pressure whatsoever upon living tissue | The skin is impermeable to pressure | Atmospheric pressure acts exclusively at night
